#### Article 9

Neglect as referred to in Article 5 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of the child properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;  
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after the child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;  
c. acts that neglect a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.  

8­

#### Article 10

Exploitation as referred to in Article 5 letter e is caused by:
a. acts that exploit economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;  
b. acts with or without the consent of the victim which include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, exploitation of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully removing or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or exploiting the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain benefits whether material or immaterial; and/or
c. any form of exploitation of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, including but not limited to all activities of prostitution or sexual abuse.

#### Article 1l

Other violence as referred to in Article 5 letter f is caused by:
a. threat of violence, including any unlawful act in the form of speech, writing, images, symbols, or body movements, whether with or without using means that cause fear or restrict a person's fundamental freedom; and
b. coercion, including a situation where a person/victim is ordered to do something in such a way that the person does something contrary to their own will.

# CHAPTER IV

## RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

#### Article 12

Every victim has the right to:
a. have their dignity respected as a human being;  
b. obtain health and psychological recovery;  
c. determine their own decisions;  
d. obtain information;  
e. obtain special handling related to victim confidentiality;  
f. obtain restitution;  
g. obtain social rehabilitation;  
h. obtain complaint handling services;  

9 -

1. obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and/or j. obtain assistance at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of the legislation.  

#### Article 13

( 1) Children as victims of violence, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 12, also obtain special rights as follows:
a. the right to respect for survival, growth and development;  
b. the right to basic services;  
c. the right to equal protection;  
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and/or
e. the right to obtain freedom.  
2. In the case of a Child as a perpetrator of sexual violence or a perpetrator of a criminal act, the child has the right to obtain legal assistance and other assistance as well as to obtain special handling related to the confidentiality of their identity.

# CHAPTER V

## OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

## Part One

Regional Government

#### Article 14

1. The Regional Government is obliged and responsible to carry out efforts to protect victims.  (2) Efforts to protect victims as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out through:
a. establishment of UPTD-PPA;  
b. policy determination;  
c. preparation of program and activity plans;  
d. provision of facilities and infrastructure support;  
e. supervision of service delivery to victims according to minimum service standards; and
f. budget allocation.  
3. In carrying out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government prepares a Regional Action Plan for the protection of victims.  
4. The Regional Action Plan for the protection of victims as referred to in paragraph (4) is stipulated by Regent Regulation.  

- 10­ Article 15 In carrying out the obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 14, the Regent grants authority to the SKPD that has duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

## Part Two

Community and Family

#### Article 16

In efforts to protect victims, the community and family have the following obligations and responsibilities:  a. prevent the occurrence of violence against women and children;  
b. report if violence occurs;  
c. protect victims; and
d. provide emergency assistance.

# CHAPTER VI

## PROTECTION OF VICTIMS

## Part One

Prevention

#### Article 17

1. Prevention of violence against women and children is carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the regional apparatus that has the main duties and functions of coordination in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.  
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph ( 1) are implemented by:
a. forming a network in efforts to prevent violence;  
b. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;  
c. providing counseling/guidance;  
d. conducting socialization about laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence; and
e. providing critical education about the rights of women and children for the community.

#### Article 18

( 1) Prevention of violence against women and children as referred to in Article I 7 is carried out by Regional Apparatus and UPTD whose main duties and functions are in the field of:
a. education;  

11­
b. health;  
c. social affairs;  
d. labor;  
e. women's empowerment and child protection; and  f. peace and order.  
2. Prevention of violence by Regional Apparatus and UPTD as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on technical guidelines in the Regional Action Plan.

## Part Two

Integrated Services for Victims

#### Article 19

1. In order to organize integrated services for Victims, the Regional Government establishes UPTD-PPA.  (2) Implementation of services to victims by UPTD-PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) may be delegated to other service units on a network basis.  (3) In carrying out services to victims as referred to in paragraph (1), UPTD-PPA is under and responsible to the Head of the PPPA Office and must coordinate with related fields.  

(4] Further provisions regarding the establishment and working procedures of UPTD-PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) are regulated by Regent Regulation.

#### Article 20

1. Services to victims as referred to in Article 19, include:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;  
b. assistance services;  
c. health services;  
d. social rehabilitation services;  
e. legal aid services;  
f. psychological services; and
g. repatriation and social reintegration services.  
2. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph ( 1) are carried out by UPTD-PPA and may cooperate with related Regional Apparatus and the community.

